<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Widukind</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 777–785</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Wittekind</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Widukind was a Westphalian nobleman who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a>. Taking refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> advanced, he returned to lead repeated revolts, including the victory at the Süntel in 782 that was followed by Charlemagne's massacre of Saxons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. In 785 he submitted and was baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attigny">Attigny</a> with Charlemagne as godfather, and later legend made him an ancestor of the Ottonian kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/widukind.jpg" alt="Widukind" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nig_Widukind_von_Sachsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
